**File:** `components/screens-components/ai-chatbot.tsx:91–136`

`pushMessage` is an `async` function. If the component unmounts while `sendAiChatMessageMock` is awaited, the `setConversationId`, `setMessages`, and `setIsSending` calls after the `await` execute on an unmounted component. In React Native this does not crash (unlike old class component `setState`), but it is a resource leak and will trigger "Can't perform a React state update on an unmounted component" warnings once real network calls are in place.

```tsx
// PROBLEM — no unmount guard
async function pushMessage(text: string) {
  ...
  setIsSending(true);
  try {
    const response = await sendAiChatMessageMock(...);  // yields here
    setConversationId(response.conversationId);          // fires on unmounted component
    setMessages((current) => [...current, response.reply]);
  } finally {
    setIsSending(false);
  }
}
```

**Fix:** Use an `isMounted` ref or an `AbortController`.

```tsx
const isMountedRef = useRef(true);
useEffect(() => {
  isMountedRef.current = true;
  return () => { isMountedRef.current = false; };
}, []);

async function pushMessage(text: string) {
  ...
  try {
    const response = await sendAiChatMessageMock(...);
    if (!isMountedRef.current) return;
    setConversationId(response.conversationId);
    setMessages((current) => [...current, response.reply]);
  } finally {
    if (isMountedRef.current) setIsSending(false);
  }
}
```

